\noindent Phase~9 is under way: its ABI and register-allocation stages have
landed and the compiler self-hosts and auto-links; diagnostics, DWARF, and the
remaining language and optimization work are what stand between it and
``production-ready'' (\S\ref{sec:goforward}).

Total: 45+ \texttt{.weft} source files (growing to ${\sim}55$ by
Phase~9), approximately 30 tangled assembly files, producing a
single native \texttt{tmp/kcc} binary that compiles C17 to ARM64
Mach-O --- with no dependency on any existing C compiler.

\section{Production Readiness}
\label{sec:production-readiness}

``Is \kcc{} a \emph{100\% functional, production-ready} C compiler?''
splits into two independent questions.  The first is \textbf{language
conformance}: does it accept and correctly translate all of C17?  The
second is \textbf{production engineering}: optimization, portability,
tooling, and robustness---everything a team depends on \emph{beyond} a
correct answer.  \kcc{} scores very differently on the two, and being
honest about the gap matters more than any single headline number.

\paragraph{What is already solid (and verified).}
The integer, pointer, and aggregate core of C is complete and, crucially,
\emph{checked against an oracle}: the differential harness
(\S\ref{sec:differential-testing}) compiles 155 programs with both
\kcc{} and \texttt{clang} and finds \emph{zero} miscompilations.  That
core includes all control flow (\texttt{if}/\texttt{while}/\texttt{do}/%
\texttt{for}/\texttt{switch}/\texttt{goto}), functions and recursion,
pointers and pointer-to-pointer, arrays (including multi-dimensional),
structs (field access, nesting, both pass and return by value, and now a
byte-accurate \stdref{6.7.2.1} layout with correct \texttt{sizeof},
padding, and alignment),
enumerations, function pointers, \texttt{typedef}, \texttt{static}
locals, initializer lists, the object- and function-like preprocessor
with source-relative \texttt{\#include}, multi-file compilation, and
now \texttt{double} floating point.

\paragraph{Language conformance --- what remains.}
The remaining language gaps are individually small but collectively keep
\kcc{} short of full C17:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcl}
\hline
Feature & Status & Note \\
\hline
standard struct layout           & \checkmark & padding/align, 15 diff. tests \\
\texttt{double} floating point   & \checkmark & complete, 14 diff. tests \\
single-precision \texttt{float}  & \checkmark & rounded at boundaries; 6 diff. tests \\
\texttt{long double}             &            & not lowered \\
\texttt{union}                   & \checkmark & offset-0 layout; 6 diff. tests \\
bit-fields                       & \checkmark & LSB-first packing; 7 diff. tests \\
\texttt{\_Bool} 0/1 normalisation & \checkmark & any non-zero $\to$ 1 \\
\texttt{char s[] = "..."}        & \checkmark & byte-copy init; 8 diff. tests \\
designated initialisers          & $\circ$    & single-level \texttt{[i]=}/\texttt{.f=}; nested TODO \\
\texttt{volatile} semantics      &            & parsed, not honoured \\
variadic \emph{definitions}      & \checkmark & \texttt{va\_arg} builtins, 3 diff. tests \\
\texttt{\_Generic} lowering      & $\circ$    & parsed, not selected \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The struct layout gap that used to hide behind otherwise-correct
results---every field on its own 8-byte slot, so
\texttt{sizeof(struct \{int x; int y;\})} was~16---is now \textbf{closed}.
\texttt{\_kcc\_type\_layout} lays aggregates out byte-accurately with
standard padding and alignment, addressing became byte-granular
(byte-offset \texttt{IR\_GEP}, real array/pointer strides, width- and
sign-correct narrow loads and stores), and \texttt{sizeof} now matches the
Standard.  What remains for \texttt{clang} object-file interoperability is
narrower: the AAPCS64 rules for \emph{passing} small structs packed into
\texttt{x0}--\texttt{x1} rather than by hidden pointer, plus the
\texttt{d0}--\texttt{d7} floating-point argument registers.  The in-memory
layout itself is now the platform layout.

\paragraph{Production engineering --- what remains.}
Once the larger axis, this is now mostly closed; what remains is
quality-oriented---an optimizer, global register allocation, input
hardening, and a second target---rather than missing capability:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcl}
\hline
Concern & Status & Note \\
\hline
register allocation          & \checkmark & linear-scan; cross-block in loop-free \& loopy functions \\
optimizer / CSE / FP r.a.    & $\circ$ & const-fold+DCE run; CSE/copy-prop/FP-alloc left \\
$>8$ arguments on the stack  & \checkmark & self-consistent, 5 diff. tests \\
AAPCS64 register convention  & \checkmark & FP/small+large struct/x8; interop 16/16 \\
second target (x86-64/ELF)   &         & ARM64 + Mach-O only \\
automatic linking (\texttt{ld}) & \checkmark & \texttt{kcc x.c -o x}; \texttt{just e2e} \\
freestanding sys.\ headers   & \checkmark & built-in \texttt{<stdio.h>} etc.; GOT-indirect data \\
multi-error recovery         & \checkmark & reports every error; carets; \texttt{just diagtest} \\
warnings (\texttt{-Wall})    & \checkmark & 5 canonical warnings; \texttt{just warntest} \\
debug info (DWARF)           & \checkmark & \texttt{-g}; func-granularity; verify-clean + links \\
harden vs.\ malformed input  & $\circ$ & crash classes fixed (lexer/output/frame); large-symbol perf left \\
full \texttt{-O}/\texttt{-I}/\texttt{-D} CLI & $\circ$ & partial \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\paragraph{Bottom line.}
As a \emph{correct compiler for the integer/pointer/aggregate subset of
C, plus \texttt{double} and single-precision \texttt{float}}, \kcc{} is in
good shape and unusually well-tested for a hand-written assembly project:
the differential suite finds zero miscompilations against \texttt{clang}
(187/0), the mixed-object interoperability suite is green in both
directions (16/16), and the compiler self-hosts to a byte-identical binary.
As a \emph{complete C17 implementation} it is roughly four-fifths there:
with \texttt{union}s, single-precision \texttt{float}, bit-fields, single-level
designated initializers, \texttt{\_Bool} normalization, and
\texttt{char s[] = "..."} now done, the bounded pieces still missing are
\texttt{long double}, \texttt{\_Generic}, \texttt{volatile} semantics, nested
designator chains, and per-operation single-precision float rounding (today
\texttt{float} arithmetic rounds only at conversion boundaries).

The toolchain axis, previously the weakest, has closed most of its gaps.
Standard struct layout, more-than-eight stack arguments, variadic function
definitions, and a fully AAPCS64-compliant calling convention (floating values
in \texttt{d} registers, small structs in \texttt{x0}--\texttt{x1},
9--16-byte struct arguments in two registers, large struct returns via
\texttt{x8}) are done and cross-checked against \texttt{clang}.  The driver
links automatically---\texttt{kcc hello.c -o hello} produces a runnable
executable in one step (\S\ref{sec:auto-linking})---serving freestanding system
headers (\texttt{\#include <stdio.h>} yields real prototypes, imported data
reached GOT-indirect).  The front end now recovers past the first error and
reports every diagnostic with a \texttt{clang}-style caret (\texttt{just
diagtest}), \texttt{-Wall} enables five canonical warnings (\texttt{just
warntest}), a linear-scan register allocator uses both caller- and
callee-saved banks and keeps values in registers across blocks in loop-free
functions, and \texttt{-g} emits verify-clean, linkable DWARF
(Chapter~\ref{ch:dwarf}).  Ad-hoc fuzzing also closed the front end's crash
classes (truncated input, oversized output, large frames).

What still separates \kcc{} from an \emph{industrial} toolchain is now
narrow and quality-oriented rather than a matter of missing capability:
there is no IR optimizer, register allocation does not yet cover loop-carried
or floating values, so generated code is correct but not fast; and there is a
single target (ARM64/Mach-O).  The shortest credible path from here runs
through extending register allocation to loop-carried and floating values,
then IR optimization passes (CSE, copy propagation, strength reduction) for
code quality.  Everything after that---a second target, statement-level DWARF,
an optimizer rivalling \texttt{clang -O1}---is refinement rather than
feasibility.
